{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01J4DwUkEda6mMddBmfsAz95","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Differential-verification briefing (read fully)\n2\t\n3\t`$W = /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-s3DwgU` (the working dir).\n4\t\n5\tWe are verifying that the Rust crate in `$W/translation` is a byte-exact\n6\ttranslation of the C libsodium in `$W/c_src/libsodium`.\n7\t\n8\t**The C is ground truth and is ALWAYS correct.** Never \"fix\" the C. Never modify\n9\tanything under `$W/c_src/`. Fix only the Rust.\n10\t\n11\t## Already built for you\n12\t\n13\t* C shared library: `$W/c_src/build/libsodium.so` (already built; do not rebuild\n14\t  unless it is missing: `cd $W/c_src/build && cmake --build . -j8`).\n15\t* Rust cdylib: `cd $W/translation && cargo build --release --offline`\n16\t  → `target/release/liblibsodium.so`. `cargo check` is clean already.\n17\t* Build config being reproduced: **no `HAVE_*` macros at all** (equivalent to\n18\t  `configure --disable-asm`): no TI_MODE, no NATIVE_LITTLE_ENDIAN, no SIMD,\n19\t  no pthreads. To see exactly what the C compiler sees, run:\n20\t  `HARVEST_WORKDIR=$W bash $W/tools/cpp.sh $W/c_src/libsodium/<path>/<file>.c`\n21\t  (the `HARVEST_WORKDIR=` prefix is required). **That output is the ground truth**\n22\t  for which branch is compiled and for the final linker names (many internal\n23\t  names are `#define`d to `_sodium_`-prefixed symbols by\n24\t  `include/sodium/private/quirks.h`).\n25\t\n26\t## Test harness (already written — DO NOT EDIT IT)\n27\t\n28\t`$W/translation/tests/common/mod.rs` loads BOTH `.so`s with `libloading` and\n29\tcalls `sodium_init()` on each. Never call Rust functions directly; always go\n30\tthrough the loaded `.so`. Example (see `$W/translation/tests/smoke.rs`):\n31\t\n32\t```rust\n33\t#[macro_use]\n34\tmod common;\n35\tuse core::ffi::c_int;\n36\t\n37\t#[test]\n38\tfn sha256() {\n39\t    // both!(symbol_name, fn type) -> (c_fn, rust_fn)\n40\t    let (c, r) = both!(\"crypto_hash_sha256\",\n41\t                       unsafe extern \"C\" fn(*mut u8, *const u8, u64) -> c_int);\n42\t    let mut rng = common::Rng::new(0xC0FFEE);      // deterministic splitmix64\n43\t    for n in [0usize, 1, 63, 64, 65, 1000] {\n44\t        let msg = rng.bytes(n);\n45\t        let (mut co, mut ro) = ([0u8; 32], [0u8; 32]);\n46\t        let rc = unsafe { c(co.as_mut_ptr(), msg.as_ptr(), n as u64) };\n47\t        let rr = unsafe { r(ro.as_mut_ptr(), msg.as_ptr(), n as u64) };\n48\t        assert_eq!(rc, rr, \"return code\");            // same error/success code\n49\t        common::eqb(&format!(\"sha256 n={n}\"), &co, &ro); // byte-identical output\n50\t    }\n51\t}\n52\t```\n53\t\n54\tHarness API: `common::libs()`, macros `both!`, `getsym!`, `both_data!`,\n55\t`common::Rng{new,next_u64,u8,below,fill,bytes}`, `common::eqb`, `common::eqi`,\n56\t`common::hex`.\n57\t\n58\tHelpful extras you may need (write them in YOUR OWN test file, not in common):\n59\t* Opaque state structs: allocate `vec![0u8; statebytes]` where `statebytes`\n60\t  comes from the C `crypto_*_statebytes()` export, and check the C and Rust\n61\t  `statebytes()` agree. Compare the **whole state buffer** byte-for-byte after\n62\t  `init`/`update` where the C state is a plain struct (this catches padding-free\n63\t  layout divergence); if a state legitimately contains uninitialised padding,\n64\t  compare only the final digest/output and say so in a comment.\n65\t* When output length is variable, always also fill the output buffer with a\n66\t  distinct canary pattern in both calls and compare the FULL buffer, so\n67\t  over-writes / under-writes are caught.\n68\t\n69\t## Rules\n70\t\n71\t1. Run tests as:\n72\t   `cd $W/translation && timeout 600 cargo test --release --offline --target-dir $W/_t/<AREA> --test <AREA> 2>&1 | tail -40`\n73\t   (use YOUR OWN `--target-dir` so parallel agents don't fight over the lock).\n74\t   `--release` is mandatory (the crate needs `overflow-checks = false`).\n75\t   Note `panic = \"abort\"`: a failing assert aborts the test binary, so run\n76\t   with `-- --test-threads=1` if you need to see which case failed first.\n77\t2. Do **not** modify: `$W/c_src/**`, `translation/Cargo.toml`,\n78\t   `translation/tests/common/mod.rs`, `translation/src/lib.rs`, or any Rust\n79\t   `src/*.rs` module outside your assigned list. If you find a divergence that\n80\t   is rooted in a module you do not own, STILL report it precisely (function,\n81\t   input, C vs Rust output) in your final report and mark the row as FAILING\n82\t   rather than silently skipping it.\n83\t3. Never `panic!`/`unwrap` in library code; never stub a Rust function to make a\n84\t   test pass. Fix the real logic to match the C.\n85\t4. Randomized, property-style testing with a FIXED seed. Many inputs per\n86\t   configuration row (≥20 random cases per row where cheap; ≥3 for expensive\n87\t   ones like pwhash). Include boundary sizes: 0, 1, block-1, block, block+1,\n88\t   multi-block, and sizes that straddle internal buffer boundaries.\n89\t5. Error paths must match the **exact** return value (`-1`, `0`, `NULL`,\n90\t   specific errno-like codes), not merely \"both failed\". Include:\n91\t   null pointers where the C tolerates them, zero/oversized lengths, values one\n92\t   past a valid range, and **out-of-range enum values** (e.g. an `alg` or `tag`\n93\t   int with no valid variant) — C enums accept any `int`.\n94\t   If a C path calls `sodium_misuse()`/`abort()`, do NOT test it in-process\n95\t   (it would kill the test binary); instead note it in the errors table with\n96\t   \"abort — not testable in-process\" and verify by inspection that the Rust\n97\t   does the same.\n98\t\n99\t## Deliverables (all three are mandatory)\n100\t\n101\t1. `$W/translation/tests/<AREA>.rs` — the differential tests, all passing.\n102\t2. `$W/_v/configs/<AREA>.md` — CONFIGURATION-SURFACE table fragment: markdown\n103\t   rows only (no header), one row per meaningful combination of\n104\t   options/modes/flags × input shapes that the C actually branches on, in the\n105\t   format (last column `[x]` once its randomized test passes):\n106\t\n107\t   `| <area>-1 | crypto_foo_bar, crypto_foo_bar_final | key=NULL, outlen=16, 3 chunked updates | [x] |`\n108\t\n109\t   Cover the FULL set of public entry points in your area, including the\n110\t   low-level/streaming ones, not just one-shot wrappers.\n111\t3. `$W/_v/errors/<AREA>.md` — ERROR-SURFACE table fragment: markdown rows only,\n112\t   one row per DISTINCT rejection site found by grepping your C files for\n113\t   `return -1`, `return NULL`, `goto`-to-error, `sodium_misuse`, `assert`,\n114\t   explicit range/size checks, `_MIN`/`_MAX` constants, etc. Format:\n115\t\n116\t   `| <area>-E1 | crypto_foo_bar | outlen > crypto_foo_bytes_max | returns -1 | [x] |`\n117\t\n118\t   Every row needs a test in your `.rs` file (except abort/misuse rows, marked\n119\t   `[abort]`).\n120\t\n121\tDerive rows mechanically from the C source. Do not guess, do not skip the\n122\t\"unimportant looking\" ones. Aim for completeness over brevity.\n123\t\n124\t## Final report\n125\t\n126\tReport: (a) counts — config rows, error rows, tests, all passing yes/no;\n127\t(b) every Rust divergence you found, with the exact input and the fix;\n128\t(c) anything you could NOT verify and why. Be honest — do not claim success for\n129\twork you did not run.\n130\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Apng92FW5xSe5pjrvLy8cb","session_id":"f6aed658-f045-47ac-a150-aec5e315d5dc","uuid":"fb3f4775-4172-493e-b8fe-9f84e59c19d3","timestamp":"2026-09-04T03:27:38.281Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Verify hash/sha3/xof area"}
