{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_phldrlze6emxdlukj3qnln4w22ayx5k6l5gexq2aywn7pnribhya","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All deliverables complete. Final summary:\n\n## (a) Counts\n\n| Item | Count |\n|---|---|\n| Config rows (`_v/configs/ed25519low.md`) | **65**, all `[x]` |\n| Error rows (`_v/errors/ed25519low.md`) | **47** — 38 `[x]`, 2 `[abort]`, 7 `[unreachable]` |\n| Rust tests (`translation/tests/ed25519low.rs`, 2042 lines) | **17** |\n| All passing | **yes** — `17 passed; 0 failed` |\n| Exported symbols covered | **71 / 71** (21 `crypto_core_ed25519_*`, 20 `crypto_core_ristretto255_*`, 30 `_sodium_{fe,ge,sc,ristretto}25519_*`) |\n\nRun with:\n```\ncd $W/translation && cargo build --release --offline --target-dir $W/_t/ed25519low   # required first\ntimeout 600 cargo test --release --offline --target-dir $W/_t/ed25519low --test ed25519low\n```\n(the first step matters: `cargo test --test <AREA>` does **not** build the `cdylib` into a custom `--target-dir`, so without it `tests/common/mod.rs` silently falls back to `translation/target/release/liblibsodium.so`.)\n\n## (b) Rust divergences found\n\n**None.** Every one of the 71 exported symbols matched the C byte-for-byte, including all raw `fe25519 = int32_t[10]` limb representations inside `ge25519_p2`/`p3`/`p1p1` outputs (not just canonical encodings), all return codes, and the partially-written structs left behind on `ge25519_frombytes` / `ristretto255_frombytes` failure paths (compared against a shared canary fill).\n\nBecause a clean pass is a weak signal, I validated the harness itself with two mutation tests:\n- flipping the result of `_sodium_sc25519_is_canonical` → 3 tests failed;\n- XOR-ing one limb in `_sodium_sc25519_mul` → 4 tests failed, **including** `sc25519_invert_and_is_canonical`, which proves the `extern \"C\"` cross-module call in `ed25519_ref10_sc.rs` resolves to the Rust `.so`'s own `_sodium_sc25519_mul` and is not interposed by the C library.\n\nBoth mutations were reverted (verified by grep and by the tests passing again). No `src/*.rs` file was permanently modified.\n\nTests also carry anti-vacuity assertions (`Seen::require`) so a corpus that stops reaching a branch fails loudly: both `0` and `-1`/`1` are asserted to occur for `ge25519_frombytes`, `frombytes_negate_vartime`, `is_canonical`, `is_on_curve`, `is_on_main_subgroup`, `has_small_order`, `sc25519_is_canonical`, `ristretto255_frombytes`, both `is_valid_point`s, both `add`/`sub` pairs, both `scalar_invert`s, both `scalar_is_canonical`s, and all five `*_from_string`. The three OR'd sub-conditions of `ristretto255_frombytes`'s single `return -(...)` are counted individually (non-square=11, `isnegative(T)`=27, `iszero(Y)`=2) and each asserted > 0.\n\n## (c) What I could not verify\n\n1. **7 `[unreachable]` error rows** — `ge25519_is_on_curve(...) == 0` after a *successful* `ge25519_frombytes` (E17, E21, E23, E25, E27) is provably dead: `frombytes` solves the curve equation for x with Z=1, and both the `vx²==u` and `vx²==-u` (×√-1) branches yield an on-curve point. Likewise `crypto_core_ed25519_add != 0` inside `from_string` (E32) is dead because both operands come from `ge25519_from_hash`. Verified by inspection in both C and Rust; the shared `return 0`/`return -1` statements are covered by the neighbouring rows.\n2. **2 `[abort]` rows** — `_string_to_points(n > 2)` (unreachable: only n=1/n=2 callers) and `assert(h_len <= 0xff)` in `core_h2c` (h_len is only ever 48/64/96). Confirmed by inspection that the Rust calls `crate::csys::abort()` at both sites; the third abort, `ge25519_elligator2`'s `xmont_to_ymont != 0`, is unreachable by construction and also mirrored in `ed25519_ref10_ristretto.rs`.\n3. **RNG-driven functions** (`crypto_core_ed25519_random`, `crypto_core_ristretto255_random`, both `scalar_random`) — bytes are not comparable, so I only assert return codes and structural validity, cross-validating each library's output with the *other* library's `is_valid_point` / `scalar_is_canonical`, plus the `&= 0x1f` / non-zero / canonical invariants of `scalar_random`.\n4. **Static-only helpers** (`ge25519_p3_to_cached`, `p3_to_precomp`, `add_precomp`/`sub_precomp` — the C's names for the brief's `madd`/`msub` — `add_cached`/`sub_cached`, `p2_dbl`, `p3_dbl`, `p3p3_dbl`, `p3_neg`, `p3_dbladd`, `mul_l`, `cmov8*`, `equal`, `negative`, `slide_vartime`, `fe25519_sqrt`, `notsquare`, `pow22523`, `reduce64`, `sqrt_ratio_m1`, `elligator*`) are not exported so cannot be called directly; they are exercised indirectly through `scalarmult`, `scalarmult_base`, `double_scalarmult_vartime`, `p3_add/sub`, `clear_cofactor`, `is_on_main_subgroup`, `from_uniform/from_hash` and the ristretto entry points, with full internal limb comparison.\n5. **Note on ownership:** the Rust puts `_sodium_core_h2c_string_to_hash` (C: `core_h2c.c`, a different object file) inside my assigned `src/core_ed25519.rs`. I did not duplicate its testing — `_v/configs/h2c.md` already covers it exhaustively — but flagging it because a fix for that symbol would land in a file I own."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":853,"cache_read_input_tokens":195641,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":853,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01PSgpMy6FgWrLHfB3wSRkpp","session_id":"f6aed658-f045-47ac-a150-aec5e315d5dc","uuid":"251406b2-393c-4d03-a751-f243f38d9e21","timestamp":"2026-09-04T03:49:16.642Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Verify ed25519 ref10 internals"}
