{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_nmorfuauunkwpm2gboxjorjo5ipa3glodwjluyrljkqgrvtz2hkq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Final state verified.\n\n## Report\n\n### (a) Counts\n\n| Item | Count |\n|---|---|\n| Config rows (`_v/configs/box.md`) | **59**, all `[x]` |\n| Error rows (`_v/errors/box.md`) | **49** → 38 `[x]`, 8 `[abort]`, 3 `[n/a]` (unreachable `LCOV_EXCL` branches) |\n| Test functions (`translation/tests/box.rs`, 3261 lines) | **16** |\n| Result | **all 16 passing**, 4.3 s |\n\nExported symbols from my object files: **108** (per `_v/persym.txt`) — every one is exercised, including all five internal `_sodium_mlkem768_ref_*` entry points (driven through the same full matrix as the public `crypto_kem_mlkem768_*` wrappers, plus a wrapper-vs-ref pass-through check inside each library).\n\nRun command:\n```\ncd $W/translation && cargo build --release --offline --target-dir $W/_t/box \\\n && cargo test --release --offline --target-dir $W/_t/box --test box -- --test-threads=1\n```\nNote: `cargo test --test box` alone does **not** rebuild the cdylib (the crate is `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]`, so the integration test does not depend on the lib target). `cargo build --release` must be run first or the test loads a stale `liblibsodium.so`. This is a trap for anyone re-running these tests.\n\n### (b) Rust divergences found\n\n**None.** `box_.rs`, `kx.rs`, `kem.rs` and `kem_mlkem768_ref.rs` are byte-exact against the C for every configuration and error row above, with no source changes required.\n\nTo prove the tests are not vacuous I ran a negative control: injecting two single-token bugs (`barrett_reduce`'s `3329` → `3328` in `kem_mlkem768_ref.rs`, and `keys[i]` → `keys[i] ^ 1` in `crypto_kx_client_session_keys`) made 5 of the 16 tests fail immediately. Both edits were reverted and the pristine line counts (179 / 994) and constants re-verified; the full suite passes again.\n\nCoverage highlights: byte-exact `*_seed_keypair`/`beforenm`/`easy`/`detached`/`afternm`/low-level zero-padded API for both primitives; single-bit tampering at **every** byte of the ciphertext and MAC (box, seal, and all 1088 ML-KEM / 1120 X-Wing ciphertext bytes); every short `clen` in `0..MACBYTES`, `0..ZEROBYTES`, `0..SEALBYTES`; the ML-KEM non-canonical-coefficient boundary (3328 accepted / 3329, 3330, 4095 rejected) at 9 coefficient indices plus a single-bit sweep over all 1184 public-key bytes; ML-KEM's implicit-rejection path producing identical pseudorandom shared secrets in both libraries; `crypto_kx` with `rx == NULL`, `tx == NULL` and `rx == tx` (including the subtle store-order aliasing, which differs between the client and server functions); low-order/zero X25519 public keys (0, 1, p, p+1, two order-8 points) at every site that calls `crypto_scalarmult_curve25519`.\n\n### (c) Not verified\n\n1. **`sodium_misuse()` paths (8 rows, `[abort]`)** — `mlen > MESSAGEBYTES_MAX` in the six `easy`/`easy_afternm`/`seal` entry points, and `rx == NULL && tx == NULL` in both `crypto_kx_*_session_keys`. These `abort()`, which would kill the test binary, and `MESSAGEBYTES_MAX` is `SIZE_MAX - 16` so the buffer cannot be allocated anyway. Verified by inspection that the Rust uses the identical guard (`mlen > SODIUM_SIZE_MAX - 16`) and the same three NULL checks in the same order.\n2. **3 unreachable `LCOV_EXCL` branches (`[n/a]`)** — `crypto_box_seal`/`crypto_box_curve25519xchacha20poly1305_seal` returning `-1` when `keypair()` fails (`crypto_scalarmult_curve25519_base` never fails), and `crypto_kem_xwing_dec` returning `-1` when `crypto_kem_mlkem768_dec()` fails (ML-KEM `dec` unconditionally returns 0). Not triggerable from the public API; verified by inspection that the Rust has the same branches.\n3. **`randombytes`-driven functions** (`*_keypair`, `crypto_kem_*_enc`, `*_seal`) cannot be compared byte-for-byte because each `.so` has an independent RNG. These are covered by return-code equality plus cross-library round trips in both directions (C-encrypt → Rust-decrypt and Rust-encrypt → C-decrypt), and for X-Wing additionally by checking that the C-generated `sk` re-expands to the C-generated `pk` under the Rust `seed_keypair`.\n4. `kem_mlkem768_wrapper_matches_ref` is an intra-library structural test (wrapper output == `ref` output), not a differential one — it passed even in the negative control, by design. The differential coverage for those symbols comes from `kem_mlkem768_public_api` and `kem_mlkem768_ref_internals`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":386,"cache_read_input_tokens":225517,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":386,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Y7qGRT4pHwRjeMu6FBk1VR","session_id":"f6aed658-f045-47ac-a150-aec5e315d5dc","uuid":"53b4a06f-7bec-46eb-ba2e-e188038e01e7","timestamp":"2026-09-04T03:53:37.853Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Verify box/kx/kem area"}
