{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_3tf36cl7rq3ujnxt3ybmjylwbiaikeuzeneae7f2kt7tnqu4z47q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cleanup done. Final state verified: 31 tests, all passing (both serial and parallel).\n\n**Summary of work**\n\n**(a) Counts**\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| `_v/configs/sodium.md` | **60** configuration rows, all `[x]` |\n| `_v/errors/sodium.md` | **99** error rows — 72 `[x]` (tested), 14 `[abort]` (unreachable/abort-only, inspected), 13 `[n/a]` (not compiled in this build / provably unreachable) |\n| `translation/tests/sodium.rs` | **31** `#[test]` functions, **all passing** (verified with `--test-threads=1` and with the default parallel harness) |\n| Symbol coverage | all **86** exported symbols from my object files are exercised (`sodium_*`, `randombytes*`, `crypto_ipcrypt_*`, `_sodium_alloc_init`, `_sodium_runtime_get_cpu_features`, `_crypto_ipcrypt_pick_best_implementation`, plus the exported data objects `randombytes_sysrandom_implementation`, `randombytes_internal_implementation`, `ipcrypt_soft_implementation`) |\n\n**(b) Divergence found and fixed — 1**\n\nThe reference C build has an **empty `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE`**, so `-DNDEBUG` is *not* defined and every C `assert()` is live. The Rust translation had dropped them all (as `// assert(...)` comments / a `debug_assert!` that compiles away under `debug-assertions = false`).\n\nOne of them is reachable through the public ABI:\n\n- **Input:** `randombytes_sysrandom_implementation.buf(ptr, 0)` (calling the exported implementation struct's function pointer directly; `randombytes_buf()` itself guards `size > 0`).\n- **C:** `randombytes_linux_getrandom(ptr, 0)` → `chunk_size = 0` → `assert(chunk_size > (size_t) 0U)` fails → **SIGABRT**.\n- **Rust (before):** returned normally (exit 0).\n- **Fix:** added a local `unsafe fn c_assert(cond: bool) { if !cond { crate::csys::abort() } }` to `translation/src/randombytes_sysrandom.rs` and `translation/src/randombytes_internal.rs`, and restored every `assert()` from those two C files (`chunk_size > 0`, `size <= 256`, `safe_read`'s `size > 0` / `size <= SSIZE_MAX`, `stream.nonce != 0`, `(getentropy_available | getrandom_available) == 0`, both `ret == 0` chacha20 checks). Also replaced the ineffective `debug_assert!(b64_pos <= b64_len)` in `translation/src/sodium_codecs.rs` with a real abort-on-failure. Verified: both libraries now raise SIGABRT for that input, and neither aborts for `randombytes_internal_implementation.buf(ptr, 0)` (no assert on that path). Regression test: `randombytes_impl_buf_zero_assert`, error row `sodium-E85`.\n\nNo other divergence was found. Everything else (`sodium_memcmp`/`compare`/`is_zero`/`increment`/`add`/`sub`/`memzero`/`stackzero`/`pad`/`unpad`/`malloc`/`allocarray`/`free`/`mlock`/`munlock`/`mprotect_*`, all of `codecs.c` including `ip2bin`/`bin2ip`, `core.c`, `runtime.c`, `version.c`, all of `randombytes*`, all of `crypto_ipcrypt`) matched byte-for-byte, including exact return values, `errno` values, out-pointer values, and untouched-buffer/canary behaviour.\n\n**Test-quality evidence (mutation testing).** To confirm the tests are discriminating rather than vacuous, I injected **53** small bugs into the nine Rust modules I own, rebuilt, and re-ran the relevant test: **44 were killed**. The 9 survivors were each analysed; 2 were mis-targeted duplicates (a shared substring made the patch hit `sodium_memcmp` instead of `sodium_is_zero`) whose corrected forms *were* killed, and the remaining **7 are provably semantically equivalent** through the public ABI — e.g. `>> 56` → `>> 48` in `sodium_pad`'s `barrier_mask` (identical for any blocksize < 2^48), `acc_len > 4` → `acc_len > 5` in `sodium_base642bin` (`acc_len ∈ {0,2,4,6}`), `size >= SIZE_MAX/count` → `size > SIZE_MAX/count` in `sodium_allocarray` (the wrapped product is always ≈ `SIZE_MAX`, so `malloc` fails with the same `NULL`/`ENOMEM`), and `ip_maxlen <= 2` → `<= 1` in `sodium_bin2ip` (the shortest possible output is `\"::\"`, so the later `len >= ip_maxlen` check returns `NULL` anyway).\n\n**Notable C behaviours pinned by the tests** (worth knowing for other areas): `sodium_bin2hex`/`sodium_bin2base64` **abort** (not `-1`) on an undersized output buffer; every out-of-range base64 `variant` (anything with `(variant & ~6) != 1`, e.g. `4`, `-1`, `99`) **aborts** via `sodium_base64_check_variant`; `sodium_unpad` writes `*unpadded_buflen_p` even when it returns `-1` for bad padding, but *not* when it rejects `padded_buflen < blocksize`; and both `sodium_hex2bin` and `sodium_base642bin` zero `bin_pos` *before* the late `hex_end`/`b64_end == NULL` `EINVAL` check, so `*bin_len` still reports the decoded count in that case.\n\n**(c) What I could not verify**\n\n- The 14 `[abort]` rows: `sodium_misuse()`/`assert()` sites that are unreachable in this build (working `getrandom(2)`, so the `/dev/urandom` fallback, `EIO` device-open failure, `poll()` failure, short-read and short-`getrandom` paths never execute) or provably unreachable arithmetically (`assert(b64_pos <= b64_len)`, `assert(ret == 0)` on chacha20, `assert(stream.nonce != 0)`). Verified by inspection that the Rust now aborts identically. Every *reachable* misuse/abort site **was** exercised, in a forked child (`assert_aborts`), covering both libraries: `sodium_pad` overflow, `sodium_bin2hex` ×2, the three `sodium_base64_check_variant` call sites × 9 bad variants, `sodium_base64_encoded_len`/`sodium_bin2base64` size overflow, `sodium_bin2base64` undersized buffer, `randombytes_buf_deterministic` oversized size, `sodium_misuse()` itself, and the E85 assert.\n- The 13 `[n/a]` rows: code that this build does not compile at all (`HAVE_ALIGNED_MALLOC` guarded-heap allocator, the `_WIN32`/pthread `sodium_crit_*` variants and their `EPERM` path, `_sodium_alloc_init`'s page-size misuse) or that is compiled but unreachable (`sodium_init`/`sodium_set_misuse_handler`'s `crit_enter/leave != 0` branches, the three `_mprotect_*` static callbacks that `_sodium_mprotect` never invokes, `randombytes_sysrandom_close`'s `-1` branch which becomes unreachable once `sodium_init()` sets `getrandom_available`).\n- RNG *values* from the real entropy source are nondeterministic, so for `randombytes_random`/`buf`/`uniform` on the real backends I only compare structure and ranges. To get exact byte comparison I install a **deterministic test implementation** (independent counter per library) via `randombytes_set_implementation` and compare a ~700-entry transcript entry-by-entry — this makes every branch of `randombytes.c` (including the `uniform == NULL` rejection-sampling loop, the `uniform != NULL` delegation, `stir == NULL`, `close == NULL`, and `implementation == NULL`) byte-exactly verifiable, and likewise makes the four `crypto_ipcrypt_*_keygen` outputs byte-comparable.\n- Pre-existing `clashing_extern_declarations` warnings in the crate (`randombytes_buf`/`sodium_memzero` declared as `*mut u8` in `sodium_utils.rs` vs `*mut c_void` elsewhere) are ABI-identical and were not introduced by me; I left them alone."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":943,"cache_read_input_tokens":324371,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":943,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_016gmjYB361KXpzzEmkbczFo","session_id":"f6aed658-f045-47ac-a150-aec5e315d5dc","uuid":"5a17d172-635a-4c3a-8496-b56f4a6f7419","timestamp":"2026-09-04T04:02:16.772Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Verify sodium utils/codecs/runtime"}
